			<div class="content" id="post-content-2290499">
									

<p>What Titanic reference are you seeing? The point is that it's the Titanic. Sorta.</p>
<p>As for it &quot;just being flipped&quot;, I encourage you to think about it a little bit more. Mental excercise: take a piece of blank paper and draw the Titanic wreckage from either memory or from online. You've got the front half of the boat at the top, the busted-to-all-hell backside at the bottom, and in the middle-right of the page you've got the major debris field.</p>
<p>Now turn it over and look at what you've drawn from underneath, through a bright light. <em>That's</em> SCP-2319. Whatever force at play either 'saw' the ship like this from 'underneath the paper', so to speak, or was trying to do <em>something</em> that went wrong and left it like this. Even the location of where 2319 lays speaks greatly to what-the-fuck-happened-here tone I was trying to convey.</p>
<p>This goes beyond the location and existence of the wreckage. Like a piece of metal becoming mildly magnet after being exposed to a big magnetic source, everything involving 2319 has this residue of whatever happened to it. It's fucked with the sealife in the area. Sometimes things only catch a whiff of it (and their controls go all screwy and inverted), sometimes people seeped in it go a little weird for a while and need to step back.</p>
<p>The central theme of 2319 for me is that it's a series of what-the-hell-this-is-impossibles. There was never a Titanic south of Australia, but here's one. It's flipped left-to-write, down to the lettering on everything, which would never be done, but it's done here. And whatever happened, every single bit of it has an echo/residue of whatever the hell caused it in the first place.</p>

							</div>

								
					
