				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/tygently" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2038009); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2038009&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606207878" alt="TyGently" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2038009)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/tygently" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2038009); return false;" >TyGently</a></span> <span class="odate time_1431266707 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">10 May 2015 14:05</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2290551">
									

<p>Some comments:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>At this time, the Foundation does not agree on the long-term survivability of SCP-2319, owing to the environment being hostile to the materials composing SCP-2319.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Awkward sentence. Maybe change it to something along the lines of &quot;SCP-2319 is expected to deteriorate gradually due to hostile environmental conditions&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Historical maps show no hints of SCP-2319 dating back decades, but the current surface terrain is as identical to the ones found at the Titanic wreckage.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It makes sense that /maps/ wouldn't show a shipwreck, if a shipwreck has just been discovered, historical maps wouldn't have anything on it. However, you could achieve the same effect by saying that there is no record of such a ship ever sailing, as that is something that would be recorded historically.</p>
<p>Also, &quot;is as identical to the ones found&quot; is very grammatically incorrect, and also wrong in the context of the skip. It's not identical, it's mirrored.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2319 is mirrored along the X axis</p>
</blockquote>
<p>As far as I know, there is no commonly accepted definition of the &quot;X axis&quot; used geographically. If I'm right, that means that this statement means pretty much nothing.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>remaining paintworks on the hull hint</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Hint isn't very clinical, and you use it three times. I would go with &quot;indicate&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>As with the Titanic wreckage, a unique ecosystem of anemones, crabs, shrimp, starfish, and rattail thrive within the wreckage of SCP-2319</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Are they identical or mirrored from the ecosystem at the original record?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>upwards translating to downward motion</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I get the port/starboard thing, because it's flipped horizontally, but if directions are flipping vertically, why didn't Titanic show up upside-down?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>air-tight safe, it contained</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That should be &quot;air-tight safe containing&quot; or replace the comma with a semicolon.</p>
<p>Okay, so, final comments. First of all, what about the corpses? Are they identical to corpses found in the real record, DNA wise? Or can they not be analyzed?</p>
<p>Also, on the subject of importing animals from the mirrorverse, there's actually an interesting chemical principle that changes things when you mirror them. Certain molecules exist in a way that they are not symmetrical, this is called &quot;optical isomerism&quot; or &quot;chirality&quot;. The molecule mirrored one way can actually have different properties than the molecule mirrored the other way. This is especially important when dealing with medicine and drugs, as drugs can have completely different (or harmful) effects when they're mirrored. So, my question is&#8230; how does that work in the mirror-verse? Is biology slightly different to work with the different optical isomers? Or are the functions of isomers changed? Like, if isomer A is harmless and isomer B of the same thing is poison, you could say that the corpses contained high levels of the deadly isomer or something, because in their universe that's the safe one. Not really a major thing, just something relevant you might want to be aware of.</p>
<p>All in all, I'm a bit iffy on this concept. It seems rather&#8230; simple, and I'm not entirely sure I get what implications are coming from the last note. My thought is that some extradimensional entity tried to save the mirrorverse Titanic, but ended up just moving it to our universe and screwing up the fabric of both universes. Anyways, that's just my two cents. I'll novote for now, but I'm confident that with some tweaking you could change that.</p>
</div>
